5. But there's another level: event filters
=============================================

This is where GitHub Actions starts becoming more interesting.

You don't necessarily say:

on:
  push:

You can say:

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

Now you're saying:

Listen for push, but only when the push is to main.

So imagine:

Developer pushes to feature/login
             ↓
         push event
             ↓
     Is branch = main?
             ↓
             NO
             ↓
       Don't run

But:

Developer pushes to main
             ↓
         push event
             ↓
     Is branch = main?
             ↓
            YES
             ↓
       Run workflow

GitHub officially calls these filters. The push event supports things like branches, branches-ignore, tags, paths, etc.

6. You can filter by files too
===============================

For example:

on:
  push:
    paths:
      - 'backend/**'

Now imagine your repository:

project/
├── backend/
├── frontend/
└── docs/

If someone changes:

backend/user.go

the workflow runs.

But if someone changes:

docs/README.md

the workflow won't run because the changed file doesn't match the configured path filter.


8. Now your event-driven question
=====================================

This is the really important part.

You asked:

"Why call GitHub Actions an event-driven automation platform? Isn't it triggered by a developer or DevOps engineer in some cases?"

Yes.

But the developer is not necessarily directly triggering the workflow.

Consider this:

Developer
    │
    │ git push
    ↓
GitHub
    │
    │ generates push event
    ↓
GitHub Actions
    │
    ↓
Workflow
    │
    ↓
Runner
    │
    ↓
Tests

The developer performed:

git push

But they didn't do:

run-my-ci-workflow()

Instead, their action caused an event, and GitHub Actions was listening for that event.

That's what makes it event-driven.

